{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Java 11 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Java 11 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nLittle Johnny Bubbles enjoys spending hours in front of his computer playing video games. His favorite game is Bubble Strike, fast-paced bubble shooting online game for two players.Each game is set in one of the N maps, each having different terrain configuration. First phase of each game decides on which map the game will be played. The game system randomly selects three maps and shows them to the players. Each player must pick one of those three maps to be discarded. The game system then randomly selects one of the maps that were not picked by any of the players and starts the game.Johnny is deeply enthusiastic about the game and wants to spend some time studying maps, thus increasing chances to win games played on those maps. However, he also needs to do his homework, so he does not have time to study all the maps. That is why he asked himself the following question: \"What is the minimum number of maps I have to study, so that the probability to play one of those maps is at least $$$P$$$\"?Can you help Johnny find the answer for this question? You can assume Johnny's opponents do not know him, and they will randomly pick maps.\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Java 11 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport java.util.Scanner;\n\npublic class I {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner s = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = s.nextLong();\n        long p = Math.round(s.nextDouble() * 10000);\n        solve(n, p);\n    }\n\n    public static void solve(long n, long p) {\n        for (long studied = 0; ; studied++) {\n            long notStudied = n - studied;\n\n            long zero = notStudied >= 3 ? notStudied * (notStudied - 1) * (notStudied - 2) / 6 : 0;\n            long one = (studied > 0 && notStudied >= 2) ? studied * notStudied * (notStudied - 1) / 2 : 0;\n            long two = (studied >= 2 && notStudied > 0) ? studied * (studied - 1) * notStudied / 2 : 0;\n            long three = studied >= 3 ? studied * (studied - 1) * (studied - 2) / 6 : 0;\n\n            long thisP = one + two * 2 + three * 2;\n            long totalTriples = n * (n - 1) * (n - 2) / 6;\n\n            if (thisP * 5000 >= p * totalTriples) {\n                System.out.println(studied);\n                return;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\nimport java.util.Scanner;\n\npublic class I {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner s = new Scanner(System.in);\n        long n = s.nextLong();\n        double p = s.nextDouble();\n        solve(n, p);\n    }\n\n    public static void solve(long n, double p) {\n        for (long studied = 0; ; studied++) {\n            long notStudied = n - studied;\n\n            // Calculate total number of possible triples\n            long totalTriples = n * (n - 1) * (n - 2) / 6;\n            if (totalTriples == 0) totalTriples = 1; // avoid division by zero\n\n            // Calculate favorable cases where at least one studied map is selected\n            // Case 1: exactly 1 studied map in the triple\n            long oneStudied = (studied >= 1 && notStudied >= 2) ? \n                studied * (notStudied * (notStudied - 1) / 2) : 0;\n            \n            // Case 2: exactly 2 studied maps in the triple\n            long twoStudied = (studied >= 2 && notStudied >= 1) ? \n                (studied * (studied - 1) / 2) * notStudied : 0;\n            \n            // Case 3: exactly 3 studied maps in the triple\n            long threeStudied = (studied >= 3) ? \n                (studied * (studied - 1) * (studied - 2) / 6) : 0;\n\n            // For each case, calculate probability that at least one studied map is played\n            // Case 1 (1 studied, 2 not): studied map is played if both players discard the same not-studied map\n            //   Each player picks one to discard. For the studied map to be played, both must discard the same not-studied map.\n            //   Probability = 1/3 (both discard same not-studied map)\n            long favorable1 = oneStudied; // each such triple contributes 1/3 chance\n            \n            // Case 2 (2 studied, 1 not): studied map is played if at least one player doesn't discard a studied map\n            //   The not-studied map is discarded only if both players pick it. Otherwise, one of the studied maps is played.\n            //   Probability = 1 - (1/3)*(1/3) = 8/9? No, let's reconsider.\n            //   Actually: each player picks one of 3 maps to discard. The system picks randomly from maps not discarded by anyone.\n            //   With 2 studied (S1,S2) and 1 not (N):\n            //   - If both discard N: system picks randomly between S1,S2 -> studied map is played (prob=1)\n            //   - If one discards N and one discards S1: system picks randomly between S2 and remaining (S1 or N) -> need to check\n            //   Let's enumerate all 9 combinations:\n            //   Player1 discards X, Player2 discards Y. Available = {maps} - {X,Y} (if X!=Y) or {maps}-{X} (if X==Y)\n            //   If X==Y==N: available={S1,S2}, system picks randomly -> studied (prob=1)\n            //   If X==Y==S1: available={S2,N}, system picks randomly -> studied with prob=1/2\n            //   If X==Y==S2: available={S1,N}, system picks randomly -> studied with prob=1/2\n            //   If X=N,Y=S1: available={S2}, studied (prob=1)\n            //   If X=N,Y=S2: available={S1}, studied (prob=1)\n            //   If X=S1,Y=N: available={S2}, studied (prob=1)\n            //   If X=S2,Y=N: available={S1}, studied (prob=1)\n            //   If X=S1,Y=S2: available={N}, not studied (prob=0)\n            //   If X=S2,Y=S1: available={N}, not studied (prob=0)\n            //   So favorable outcomes: (N,N), (N,S1), (N,S2), (S1,N), (S2,N) = 5 out of 9\n            //   Probability = 5/9\n            \n            // Case 3 (3 studied): all maps are studied, so studied map is always played (prob=1)\n            \n            // Let's recalculate with correct probabilities:\n            double prob1 = 1.0/3.0; // case with 1 studied\n            double prob2 = 5.0/9.0; // case with 2 studied\n            double prob3 = 1.0;     // case with 3 studied\n            \n            double totalFavorableProb = (oneStudied * prob1 + twoStudied * prob2 + threeStudied * prob3) / totalTriples;\n            \n            if (totalFavorableProb >= p - 1e-9) {\n                System.out.println(studied);\n                return;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.85\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-1.311301275563892e-06, -3.683499380713329e-05, -5.602820692729438e-06, 0.0, -4.255681051290594e-05, -1.3947389561508317e-05, -9.179073458653875e-06, -0.00012492353562265635, -9.179073458653875e-06, -6.794906312279636e-06, -2.932505594799295e-05, -0.28159666061401367, -7.092700980138034e-05, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -5.960462772236497e-07, -3.576278118089249